      <p>
        Two patterns stand out. First, the graduate selection has sat in a narrow band (825, 825, then 816) even when the number of LAAs changed a lot. Second, occupation-targeted selections reach much lower scores, because only people working in the named occupations compete. A manufacturing worker with 632 points was invited on July 30, while someone outside the target groups needed 846 in the October overall round. That is why your occupation and where you work can matter as much as your total.
      </p>

      <h2 id="selections" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How to Read a Manitoba PNP Draw Notice</h2>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Skilled Worker in Manitoba selections</h3>
      <p>
        The Skilled Worker in Manitoba pathway is for people with ongoing employment in the province. Its draws come in several forms. <strong>Occupation-specific selections</strong> consider everyone working in Manitoba in named NOC 2021 unit groups or broad categories, often with no published score, because Manitoba wants all of them (for example health occupations on September 10 and October 8, and teachers and early childhood educators on October 8). <strong>Top-scoring occupational selections</strong> rank only workers in one category and publish a cutoff. The <strong>completed post-secondary study in Manitoba</strong> selection targets candidates who, or whose spouse, finished post-secondary education in the province, and the notices remind them to decline the LAA if they can't prove it with a diploma and transcript. The <strong>overall top-scoring</strong> selection, which appeared on October 22, is open to all eligible pathway profiles. Five draws between July and September also included a <strong>Francophone selection</strong> for profiles declaring French as their language of communication with a valid French test.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Strategic recruitment initiatives</h3>
      <p>
        Every draw since July has a Skilled Worker Stream selection for candidates who received an Invitation to Apply (ITA) directly from the MPNP. The notices break these down by initiative: Employer Services, Ethnocultural Communities, Francophone Community, Regional Communities and the Temporary Public Policy to Facilitate Work Permits for Prospective Provincial Nominee Program Candidates. Skilled Worker Overseas candidates appear only through this route in the 2026 notices: the MPNP states that Skilled Worker Stream EOIs were considered only if the person was directly invited under a strategic recruitment initiative or that temporary public policy. If you claim an ITA, you must enter a valid invitation number or you won't be selected.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">International Education Stream</h3>
      <p>
        The only International Education Stream selection between July and November 2026 was 78 LAAs for the Graduate Internship Pathway on July 16. The MPNP has told Career Employment Pathway candidates with at least six months of Manitoba work experience to move to the Skilled Worker in Manitoba pathway, where Manitoba graduates are now selected through the post-secondary study selection.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">The Express Entry line</h3>
      <p>
        Every notice ends with a count of LAAs issued to candidates who declared a valid Express Entry profile number and job seeker validation code. In the draws above, that was a share of the total, not a separate requirement. It matters because Manitoba can nominate those candidates through Express Entry (an "enhanced" nomination), which adds 600 points to their federal Comprehensive Ranking System score. Our guide to the <a href="/articles/express-entry-job-seeker-validation-code" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Express Entry job seeker validation code</a> explains where to find it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="ranking" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How the MPNP EOI Ranking Works (1,000 Points)</h2>

      <p>
        The MPNP ranks Skilled Worker and International Education Stream profiles on six factors. The first five add up to a maximum of 1,000 points, and the sixth can only subtract.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border border-slate-200 rounded-xl">
          <thead class="bg-amber-50">
            <tr><th class="p-3 text-left">Factor</th><th class="p-3 text-left">Maximum</th><th class="p-3 text-left">Key values</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="border-t border-slate-200"><td class="p-3 font-semibold">Language</td><td class="p-3">125</td><td class="p-3">First official language: 25 per band at CLB 8+, 22 at CLB 7, 20 at CLB 6, 17 at CLB 5, 12 at CLB 4; second official language at CLB 5+: 25</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-slate-200"><td class="p-3 font-semibold">Age</td><td class="p-3">75</td><td class="p-3">75 for ages 21 to 45; 40 at 20 or 46; 0 at 50 or older</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-slate-200"><td class="p-3 font-semibold">Work experience</td><td class="p-3">175</td><td class="p-3">40 to 75 for one to four or more years in the last five; 100 for being fully recognized by a Manitoba licensing body</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-slate-200"><td class="p-3 font-semibold">Education</td><td class="p-3">125</td><td class="p-3">125 for a master's or doctorate; 110 for a program of three years or more; 70 for a one-year program or trade certificate</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-slate-200"><td class="p-3 font-semibold">Adaptability</td><td class="p-3">500</td><td class="p-3">500 for Manitoba Demand (six months or more of ongoing employment in Manitoba with a long-term offer from the same employer, or a strategic initiative ITA); 200 for a close relative in Manitoba; 100 for Manitoba work or a two-year Manitoba program; plus 50 for settling outside Winnipeg</td></tr>
            <tr class="border-t border-slate-200"><td class="p-3 font-semibold">Risk</td><td class="p-3">&minus;200</td><td class="p-3">&minus;100 each for work experience or studies in another province</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The 500-point Manitoba Demand factor explains most of the cutoffs. A worker with that connection, CLB 8 in all four abilities (100), age 21 to 45 (75), four years of experience (75) and a three-year degree (110) reaches 860 before any regional bonus. Without Manitoba Demand, the same person could get at most 200 points from a Manitoba connection (such as a close relative), plus 50 for settling outside Winnipeg. Unlike the BC PNP, which scores registrations out of 200 (see our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-vs-manitoba-mpnp-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP vs Manitoba MPNP comparison</a>), Manitoba does not award points for wage.
      </p>

      <h2 id="allocation" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Manitoba's 2026 Allocation and What the Numbers Show</h2>

      <p>
        The MPNP's 2026 monthly data page says Manitoba was allocated 8,000 nominations for 2026: 6,239 in January and 1,761 more in August. Through October, the program had issued 5,841 nominations, including 1,990 enhanced (Express Entry) nominations. Skilled Worker LAAs totalled 8,530 from January to October, with the pace rising sharply after spring: 102 in January, 2,989 in July and 1,647 in October. Applications received also climbed, from 292 Skilled Worker applications in January to more than 1,100 a month from August to October.
      </p>
      <p>
        More LAAs than nominations is normal: not everyone who receives an LAA applies, and some applications are refused (599 Skilled Worker refusals through October). The MPNP says the number of LAAs in each draw depends on annual processing targets and capacity. With roughly 2,160 nominations left under the 8,000 allocation at the end of October, the remaining 2026 draws will be shaped by how many submitted applications are still waiting for a decision. The program publishes no forecast, and neither do we.
      </p>

      <h2 id="improve" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How to Improve Your Manitoba EOI Ranking</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Retake your language test.</strong> Moving from CLB 7 to CLB 8 in each ability adds 12 points, and a valid second-official-language test at CLB 5 or higher adds 25. French also opens the Francophone selection.</li>
        <li><strong>Build Manitoba Demand.</strong> Six months of ongoing employment in Manitoba with a long-term offer from the same employer is the 500-point connection that most Skilled Worker in Manitoba selections rely on.</li>
        <li><strong>Get licensed if your job is regulated.</strong> Full recognition by a Manitoba licensing body is worth 100 points, but only claim it if you have completed every step.</li>
        <li><strong>Declare every Manitoba connection.</strong> Include completed Manitoba post-secondary education for you or your spouse, which is the basis of the graduate selection.</li>
        <li><strong>Consider the regions.</strong> A destination outside Winnipeg adds 50 regional development points when combined with another Manitoba connection other than Manitoba Demand, and you must show a real link to that community.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep the profile valid.</strong> An EOI lasts one year, and draws skip profiles with an expired language test or an invalid ITA number.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="after-laa" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What Happens After a Manitoba LAA</h2>
      <p>
        An LAA is an invitation to apply, not a nomination. You have 60 days from the LAA date to submit a full application in MPNP Online and pay the $500 non-refundable fee by Visa or Mastercard credit card; the MPNP says the application must be submitted in Microsoft Edge. You must prove every claim in your EOI with documents, and the MPNP assesses your connection to Manitoba from what you upload. If you are nominated, you then apply to IRCC for permanent residence. With a valid Express Entry profile you may receive an enhanced nomination; otherwise you apply through the paper-based, non-Express Entry process. The MPNP also warns that LAA emails can land in spam folders.
      </p>
      <p>
        Comparing provinces? Track B.C.'s rounds on our <a href="/bc-pnp-draws" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP draws page</a> and estimate your B.C. score with the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a>. For the wider picture, see <a href="/articles/canada-pnp-news-2026-all-provinces" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">PNP news for all provinces in 2026</a> and <a href="/articles/how-to-get-provincial-nomination-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">how to get a provincial nomination</a>.
      </p>

